 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training is computationally expensive due to the need to pass gradients through a large pre-trained diffusion model (Stable Diffusion) for every optimization step [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the weights of the pre-trained diffusion model and the NeRF MLP in memory simultaneously [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Enables text-guided novel view synthesis and improves visual quality by leveraging strong generative priors [S1]. It is the first approach to allow coloring of the grid under diffusion model guidance [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality and alignment of the pre-trained diffusion model; may introduce hallucinations if guidance is weak or conflicting [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When high-fidelity, semantically controlled novel views are required, and computational resources allow for diffusion-based optimization.
-*   **When not to use:** When real-time rendering or fast training is required, or when no text/shape guidance is needed.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training is computationally expensive due to the iterative diffusion denoising process and backpropagation through the diffusion model [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the NeRF MLP, the diffusion model, and the autoencoder [S2].
+*   **Performance:** Achieves impressive results in text-guided 3D generation, producing high-resolution images ($512 \times 512$) from low-resolution latent features ($64 \times 64$) [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Enables text and shape guidance for 3D generation; overcomes the low-resolution limitations of standard NeRFs [S1, S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the pre-trained diffusion model; may suffer from mode collapse without proper constraints [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** When text-guided 3D generation or high-fidelity detail synthesis from sparse/low-res data is required.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When fast training or low computational resources are available; when precise photometric reconstruction is more important than generative quality.
 
 ## Background
-Latent-NeRF was introduced as an extension of NeRF to improve image rendering quality by using pre-trained models [S1]. It is the first approach to apply score distillation from a latent diffusion model to the compact latent space of a pre-trained autoencoder for NeRF optimization [S1]. This method allows for the generation of novel views guided by text and shape, achieving impressive results in generative 3D synthesis [S1].
+Latent-NeRF was introduced in November 2022 [S1]. It was inspired by advances in generative 2D computer vision, specifically the use of 2D generative models to create images of scenes [S1]. It is the first approach to allow coloring of the grid under the guidance of a pre-trained diffusion model [S2]. It builds upon the concept of score distillation sampling (SDS) used in DreamFusion [S1, S2].
 
